<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Kamakura-fu</h1><p class="st-meta">1349 CE – 1439 CE · capital Kamakura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Ashikaga branch government that ruled eastern Japan from Kamakura and repeatedly challenged the shoguns in Kyoto.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>To control the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanto-region">Kanto</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ashikaga-shogunate">Ashikaga shogunate</a> created a regional government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a>. In 1349 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-takauji">Ashikaga Takauji</a> sent his son Motouji there as Kamakura kubo, assisted by a deputy, the Kanto kanrei, an office soon held by the Uesugi family.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kubo governed the eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanto-region">Kanto</a> provinces and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutsu-province">Mutsu</a> and Dewa, with their own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> and courts, and acted almost as rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shoguns</a>. Successors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-ujimitsu">Ujimitsu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-mitsukane">Mitsukane</a> and Mochiuji resented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto's</a> authority, and tension with the Uesugi deputies led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-zenshu-rebellion">Uesugi Zenshu revolt</a> of 1416.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-mochiuji">Ashikaga Mochiuji</a> openly defied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">Shogun</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshinori">Yoshinori</a>; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eikyo-rebellion">Eikyo Rebellion</a> he was defeated and forced to kill himself in 1439. The office was revived in 1449 for his son Shigeuji, who after fighting the Uesugi moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koga-ibaraki">Koga</a> in 1455 as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/koga-kubo">Koga kubo</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kamakura-fu.jpg" alt="Kamakura-fu" width="320" height="335"><figcaption>Image: Urashimataro · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kamakura-fu.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1349 CE – 1439 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kamakura</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ashikaga Motouji, Ashikaga Ujimitsu, Ashikaga Mochiuji</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kamakura kubo, Kanto government</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1394&amp;focus=kamakura-fu" aria-label="Kamakura-fu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8211 901 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kamakura-fu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8531 1157L8441 1168M8531 1157L8519 1128"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8441 1168h0M8519 1128h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8531 1157h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8531 1157h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kamakura. <a href="/atlas?year=1394&amp;focus=kamakura-fu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
